# Nonlinear Conjugate Gradient

Nonlinear conjugate gradient is a local optimizer for smooth functions when you
can compute a gradient. It combines the negative gradient with the previous
search direction to carry information between steps. It needs only a few
vectors, making it useful when memory limits rule out storing a matrix or a long
history of curvature pairs.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nonlinear_cg/struct.NonlinearCg.html)
· [Compare solvers](../../)

  | Property    | Requirement                                             |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                                |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient` with the same vector type |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                          |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained                                           |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`            |

## When to Use It

Consider nonlinear conjugate gradient for a large, smooth problem when you want
to use gradient information while keeping memory proportional to the number of
parameters. Basin defaults to the safeguarded method of [Hager and Zhang
(2005)](#ref-hager-zhang-2005).

[Gradient descent](../gradient-descent/) is a simpler baseline that starts from
the negative gradient every iteration. [L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/)
retains several recent curvature pairs, which may justify its additional memory
on your problem. Compare their objective and gradient evaluation counts as well
as their iteration counts, since line searches can require several evaluations
per step.

The method expects consistent gradients of a smooth objective. For stochastic
gradient estimates, compare [SGD](../sgd/). For a feasible-set projection,
consider [projected gradient descent](../projected-gradient-descent/). Nonlinear
conjugate gradient does not enforce bounds or other constraints, and different
starting points can lead to different local minima.

## How It Works

The first search direction is the negative gradient. After a line search accepts
a step, the solver forms the next direction by adding a multiple of the old
direction to the new negative gradient:

$$
  \mathbf{d}_{k+1} = -\mathbf{g}_{k+1} + \beta_k \mathbf{d}_k.
$$

Here, $\mathbf{g}_k = \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_k)$ is the gradient at iteration $k$.
The update rule chooses $\beta_k$ from the gradients and the previous direction.
The default Hager–Zhang rule includes a lower bound on this coefficient to
preserve a descent property. Basin checks the computed direction and restarts
with the negative gradient if the update becomes numerically unreliable or fails
its descent test.

You can instead select `CgUpdate::PolakRibierePlus`. This uses the coefficient
from [Polak and Ribière (1969)](#ref-polak-ribiere-1969), truncated below at
zero, with a separate descent safeguard. [Gilbert and Nocedal
(1992)](#ref-gilbert-nocedal-1992) study convergence properties of this family
of nonlinear conjugate-gradient methods.

A line search chooses how far to move along each direction. Basin uses the
[Hager–Zhang line
search](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/hager_zhang/struct.HagerZhang.html)
by default. The direction update and line search are separate choices: selecting
Polak–Ribière+ leaves that line search unchanged. The convergence result in
Hager and Zhang's paper assumes the Wolfe conditions and the paper's regularity
assumptions; it does not extend automatically to an arbitrary custom step rule.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The example uses the default
Hager–Zhang update and line search, sets a gradient tolerance on the solver, and
limits iterations through the executor.

<!-- compile: solver-nonlinear-cg -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, Gradient, NonlinearCg, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Quadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![2.0 * (x[0] - 1.0), 8.0 * (x[1] + 2.0)])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = NonlinearCg::new().with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(200)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-7);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-7);
}
```

To try Polak–Ribière+, import `CgUpdate` and add
`.with_update(CgUpdate::PolakRibierePlus)` to the solver configuration.

## Practical Considerations

**Scale the parameters.** Large differences in parameter units can make a
gradient-based direction less useful and an absolute gradient tolerance harder
to interpret. Choose a threshold for your parameterization, precision, and
derivative accuracy; `1e-8` is a choice for the example above.

**Choose a stopping test.** An exactly zero gradient at a finite point with
finite cost always terminates successfully. Other gradient, step, and
cost-change tolerances are disabled by default. Configure an absolute or
relative tolerance on the solver and put iteration or evaluation budgets on the
executor. Inspect the termination report to distinguish convergence from
reaching a budget.

**Use restarts deliberately.** Safety restarts are always active. Optional
periodic restarts discard the previous direction after a chosen number of
accepted steps; configure them with `with_restart_interval(Some(k))`. They are
disabled by default, and `Some(1)` makes every direction steepest descent. The
Hager–Zhang coefficient parameter `eta` controls its update safeguard, rather
than a stopping tolerance, and has no effect on Polak–Ribière+.

**Check gradient accuracy.** The direction update uses differences between
gradients, and the line search depends on directional derivatives. If you use
[`FiniteDiff`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/numdiff/struct.FiniteDiff.html),
account for derivative error and its additional objective evaluations when
choosing tolerances and budgets.

## Basin Implementation

Basin stores the search direction and restart bookkeeping on `NonlinearCg` and
publishes the current point, objective, and gradient through `FirstOrderState`.
The method uses vector operations and O(n) memory, without constructing a
Hessian approximation. It implements the safeguarded Hager–Zhang recurrence,
rather than every heuristic of the CG_DESCENT implementation.

Accepted line-search evaluations are reused. If a line search fails along a
conjugate direction, Basin retries once from the same point along the negative
gradient. Failure along steepest descent ends the solve with `SolverFailed` and
preserves the previous current record. You can supply another search with
`NonlinearCg::with_line_search(search)`.

For Polak–Ribière+, Basin checks the candidate direction after accepting the
step and restarts if it fails the descent safeguard. This safeguard does not
provide an unconditional convergence guarantee for every custom line search.
Changing the update rule clears the direction history.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. No matrix
operations or BLAS/LAPACK provider are required. A custom line search or an
optional convergence check can impose additional capabilities. See the API's
[Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nonlinear_cg/struct.NonlinearCg.html#backends)
for the exact requirements.

